---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const postUrl = `${base}posts/teaching-noise-where-to-go/`;
---

<Base title="Preethi Ravula — Thinking that runs.">
  <!-- Sticky in-page nav -->
  <div class="site-nav">
    <span class="nav-brand"
      >Notes in progress <span class="sep">· Issue 00 · September 2026</span
      ></span
    >
    <nav aria-label="On this page">
      <span class="nav-label">On this page</span>
      <a href="#about">About</a>
      <a href="#notes">Notes</a>
      <a href="#method">Method</a>
      <a href="#topics">Topics</a>
      <a href="#issue-00">Issue 00</a>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero" aria-label="Introduction">
    <h1>Thinking that <em>runs.</em></h1>
    <p>
      Working notes on AI systems, markets, scientific computing, and causal
      reasoning—from a claim, to the evidence, to an executable example.
    </p>
    <p>
      Published when there is a problem worth unpacking—without forcing every
      question into false certainty.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Preview card -->
  <section aria-label="Latest note">
    <a class="teaser-card" href={postUrl}>
      <div class="teaser-label">Preview the first note ↓</div>
      <h3>Teaching Noise Where to Go</h3>
      <p class="teaser-sub">
        Flow matching, moving dots, and the mathematics of generation
      </p>
      <p class="teaser-meta">Post 01 · Coming soon</p>
    </a>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <!-- About -->
  <section class="bio" id="about" aria-label="About the author">
    <img src={`${base}profile.jpg`} alt="Portrait of Preethi Ravula" />
    <div>
      <h2>Preethi Ravula</h2>
      <p class="bio-loc">Mountain View, CA</p>
      <p>Builder across enterprise agent systems.</p>
      <p>
        Applied AI researcher across evaluation, causal inference, and high
        performance computing — Ph.D. &amp; M.S. in Mechanical Engineering
        (University of Maryland, College Park), B.Tech. (IIT Kharagpur) —
        focused on making machine intelligence rigorous and useful.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="ext-link" href="https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Preethi-Ravula"
          >ResearchGate ↗</a
        >
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <!-- Technical notes -->
  <section id="notes" aria-label="Technical notes in progress">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Technical notes in progress.</h2>
      <span class="index-tag">Post 01</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      Applied technical essays, learning notes, experiments, and model
      investigations—shared before they become polished posts.
    </p>

    <article id="flow-preview" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <span class="coming-soon">Coming soon</span>
      <p class="tag-label">Generative modeling</p>
      <h3 class="post-title">Teaching Noise Where to Go</h3>
      <p class="post-subtitle">
        Flow matching, moving dots, and the mathematics of generation
      </p>
      <div class="post-body">
        <p>
          An image. A voice clip. A molecule. Very different outputs — but
          underneath them is the same problem: how do you get from random
          noise to something that belongs in the real world?
        </p>
        <p>
          Flow matching learns the journey, not just the destination. It
          learns a vector field: a direction of travel for every point, at
          every moment.
        </p>
        <p>
          Take image generation for example, You start with noise and need to
          turn it into something recognizable. With Flow matching, the
          question it asks is: which way should each point move?
        </p>
        <p>
          Picture a cloud of dots drifting, bending, rearranging itself into
          a spiral. The model learns the motion that gets it there not just
          the final shape.
        </p>
        <p>
          Stringing those directions together and you get a path. Follow that
          path with an ordinary differential equation, and noise becomes
          structure.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="closing-callout">
        <p>Coming next: the math, the model, and the moving dots.</p>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <a class="back-link" href={postUrl} style="margin-bottom: 0;">
          Read and discuss this note →
        </a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <!-- Method -->
  <section class="method-lines" id="method" aria-label="How each post works">
    <p class="method-kicker">HOW EACH POST WORKS</p>
    <h2>Take a position. Map the terrain. Make it run.</h2>
    <p class="method-sub">
      Clear claims, visible sources, and reproducible examples.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <!-- Topics -->
  <section id="topics" aria-label="Topics">
    <p class="topics-eyebrow"><span class="accent">Core</span> → Field notes</p>
    <h2 class="topics-head">Core research threads</h2>
    <div class="topics-intro">
      <p>
        These threads grow out of work I've done, keep returning to and
        questions I'm still excited to pursue.
      </p>
      <p>
        <span class="lead">Different domains, one discipline:</span> making
        assumptions visible, reasoning under uncertainty, and building models
        that can be tested.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol class="topics">
      <li>
        <span class="topic-num">01</span>
        <div>
          <p class="topic-name">AI systems in production</p>
          <p class="topic-desc">
            The gap between the demo and the deployment. What changes when
            models meet users, incentives, and operational reality.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="topic-num">02</span>
        <div>
          <p class="topic-name">Markets &amp; incentives</p>
          <p class="topic-desc">
            How mechanism design, information asymmetry, and human behavior
            reshape what technology actually does.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="topic-num">03</span>
        <div>
          <p class="topic-name">Scientific computing</p>
          <p class="topic-desc">
            High performance computing, numerical methods, and the craft of
            turning equations into executable insight.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="topic-num">04</span>
        <div>
          <p class="topic-name">Causality &amp; reasoning</p>
          <p class="topic-desc">
            From correlation to causation: identifying what actually moves the
            needle and saying so precisely.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="topic-num">05</span>
        <div>
          <p class="topic-name">
            Books, art &amp; travel <span class="field-tag">Field notes</span>
          </p>
          <p class="topic-desc">
            Occasional observations from outside the technical
            stack—kept because different disciplines sharpen the questions.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <!-- Publication note -->
  <section class="pub-note" id="issue-00" aria-label="Publication note">
    <div class="pub-meta">
      <div><span class="accent">Issue 00</span></div>
      <div>September 2026</div>
      <div>Publication note</div>
    </div>
    <h2>A place to think in public</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      This notebook starts with a simple constraint: no topic is finished at
      the headline.
    </p>
    <details class="launch-note">
      <summary
        ><span class="plus">+</span>
        <span class="label-text">Read the launch note</span></summary
      >
      <div class="launch-body">
        <p>
          Most new ideas arrive already compressed: a release announcement, a
          benchmark, a market move, a striking result. Compression is useful
          for discovery, but not for understanding. The work begins when we
          ask what the claim depends on, how it connects to the wider field,
          and whether we can make the mechanism visible.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the purpose of this notebook. Each post will begin with a
          point of view, then widen into a learning map, and finally narrow
          again into an executable example. The code is not decoration; it is
          where assumptions become inspectable.
        </p>
        <p>
          The center of gravity will be AI and agentic systems, world markets
          understood through models, scientific computing, and causal AI.
          Books, art, and travel will appear more occasionally—as another way
          to notice patterns, challenge categories, and return with better
          questions.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <!-- Closing -->
  <section class="closing-block" aria-label="Closing">
    <div class="closing-lines">
      <p>Follow the argument.</p>
      <p>Inspect the assumptions.</p>
      <p><em>Run the example.</em></p>
    </div>
    <p class="editions-note">
      New editions will appear here as the notebook grows.
    </p>
  </section>

  <script>
    document.querySelectorAll(".launch-note").forEach((d) => {
      const plus = d.querySelector(".plus");
      const label = d.querySelector(".label-text");
      d.addEventListener("toggle", () => {
        if (d.open) {
          plus.textContent = "−";
          label.textContent = "Close the launch note";
        } else {
          plus.textContent = "+";
          label.textContent = "Read the launch note";
        }
      });
    });
  </script>
</Base>
